\newpage
\begin{verbatim}
% TRANSFORMATION TPrepobjToSubjRaising
< m1:CLAUSE{CLAUSErec1}
           [ I1:mu1,
             I2:mu2,
             predrel/VERBP{VERBPrec1}
                    [ mu3,
                      head/VERB{VERBrec1}[mu5],
                      I4:mu4,
                      prepobjrel/T1,
                      mu6                       
                    ],
             mu7 
           ]
>

< m :CLAUSE{CLAUSErec1}
           [ I1:mu1,
             subjrel/T3,
             I2:mu2,
             predrel/VERBP{VERBPrec1}
                    [ mu3,
                      head/VERB{VERBrec1}[mu5],
                      I4:mu4,
                      prepobjrel/T2,
                      mu6                       
                    ],
             mu7 
           ]
>

   MATCHCONDITIONS
      <
       I1: ALL (mu1, I11:rel1/..)
       I2: NOT EXIST (mu2, [ I11:rel1/..]) AND
            NOT EXIST (mu2, [subjrel/..]) 
          I11: rel1 IN AUX_presubjrels
       I4: ALL (mu4, I41:rel2/T4)
          I41: (rel2 = objrel AND T4.CAT = NP) OR
               (rel2 = partrel AND T4.CAT = PART)
      >

<
   SUBRULE (* 1. NPVAR, CNVAR *)
      < T1: VARPREPP{VARPREPPrec1}
                    [ head/PREP{PREPrec1},
                      objrel/T3 ]        >
      < T2: PREPP{PREPPrec2}
                    [ head/PREP{PREPrec1} ] >

      COMP
         <
          C1: T3.CAT IN [NPVAR, CNVAR] AND
              CLAUSErec1.voice = Passive AND
              VARPREPPrec1.stranded = false
          A1: PREPPrec2 := VARPREPPrec1;
              PREPPrec2.stranded := true
         >
      DECOMP
         <
          C1: T3.CAT IN [NPVAR, CNVAR] AND
              CLAUSErec1.voice = Passive AND
              PREPPrec2.stranded = true
          A1: VARPREPPrec1 := PREPPrec2;
              VARPREPPrec1.stranded := false
         >

   SUBRULE (* 2. NP, for idioms *)
      < T1: PREPP{PREPPrec1}
                    [ head/PREP{PREPrec1},
                      objrel/T3 ]        >
      < T2: PREPP{PREPPrec2}
                    [ head/PREP{PREPrec1} ] >

      COMP
         <
          C1: T3.CAT = NP AND 
              CLAUSErec1.voice = Passive AND
              PREPPrec1.stranded = false
          A1: PREPPrec2 := PREPPrec1;
              PREPPrec2.stranded := true
         >
      DECOMP
         <
          C1: T3.CAT = NP AND
              CLAUSErec1.voice = Passive AND
              PREPPrec2.stranded = true
          A1: PREPPrec1 := PREPPrec2;
              PREPPrec1.stranded := false
         >
>
&

\end{verbatim}
\newpage
\begin{description}
\item[* name:] TPrepobjToSubjRaising
\item[* relevant examples:]\mbox{}
\begin{enumerate}
\item no object: \\
-- was looked at x2 $\rightarrow$ x2 was looked at\\
-- was looked down upon x2  $\rightarrow$ x2 was looked down upon
\item idiom object:\\
 -- was caught sight of x2 $\rightarrow$ x2 was caught sight of
\end{enumerate}
\item[* language:] (Dutch, English, Spanish) English
\item[* kind of rule:] (M-rule, S-rule, Transferrule) M-rule
\item[* subgrammar:] XPPROPtoCLAUSE
\item[* class:] (name of rule, transformation or filter class) TC\_Objectokrules
\item[* date of creation:] 22-MAR-1988
\item[* author:] margreet sanders
\item[* copied from:] --
\item[* modification:]\mbox{}
\begin{enumerate}
\item date: ../../..\ \ \ \ \ author:
\item date: ../../..\ \ \ \ \ author:
\end{enumerate}
\item[* task:] To turn the object of a prepobj into a subject if the verb is
passive. Note that the object of a prepobj that has a locargrel or dirargrel 
can not be moved thus ($^{*}$New York was slept in).
\item[\ \ strategy:] (also: deviations from accepted proposals)
\item[\ \ rejected alternatives:]
\item[\ \ clarification of variables:]
\item[\ \ clarification of the splitting up into subrules:] one subrule for the 
case the object of the prepobj is an ordinary VAR, one for the case it is part 
of an idiom, and hence a full NP. It is unclear whether in the latter case it 
is still possible to have an NP-object in the sentence too ( something like 
$^{*}${\em swine were cast pearls before (by John)\/}, but then not with a 
dirargrel).
\item[\ \ clarification of the collapsing of rules:] (e.g. clarification of parameters)
\item[\ \ differences analytical/generative version:] none
\item[\  \ suggested semantics:]
\item[\ \ presuppositions:]\mbox{}
\begin{enumerate}
\item dictionary:\\

\item rules:\\

\end{enumerate}
\item[\ \ consequences in the grammar:]
\item[\ \ translation aspects:]\mbox{}
\begin{enumerate} 
\item within this language:\\

\item in other languages:\\

\end{enumerate}
\item[* relevant literature/documents:]
\item[\ \ remarks:]\mbox{}
\begin{enumerate}
\item problems:\\
It is well possible that more relations and categories are allowed between the 
verb and the prepobj, e.g.\ ADVs (The plan was thought better of) or even 
all non-VAR categories. Andr\'{e} will think about this.
\item modifications:\\

\end{enumerate}
\end{description}

\newpage
\begin{verbatim}
% TRANSFORMATION TThereSubjInsertion
< m1:CLAUSE{CLAUSErec1}
           [ I1:mu1,
             I2:mu2,
             predrel/VERBP{VERBPrec1}
                    [ mu3,
                      head/VERB{VERBrec1}[mu5],
                      I3:sig1,
                      I6:objrel/T1,
                      I4:mu4
                    ],
             mu7 
           ]
>

< m :CLAUSE{CLAUSErec1}
           [ I1:mu1,
             subjrel/NP{NPrec1}
                    [head/THERE(AUX_thereTHEREkey){THERErec1}],
             I2:mu2,
             predrel/VERBP{VERBPrec1}
                    [ mu3,
                      head/VERB{VERBrec1}[mu5],
                      I3:sig1,
                      I6:objrel/T1,
                      I4:mu4
                    ],
             mu7 
           ]
>

   MATCHCONDITIONS
      <
       I1: ALL (mu1, I11:rel1/..)
       I2: NOT EXIST (mu2, [ I11:rel1/..]) AND
           NOT EXIST (mu2, [subjrel/..])
          I11: rel1 IN AUX_presubjrels
       I3: ALL (sig1, partrel/..)
       I4: NOT EXIST (mu4, [partrel/..])
       I6: T1.CAT IN [NPVAR, NP]
      >

<
   SUBRULE (* 1. NPVAR *)
      < T1: NPVAR{NPVARrec1} >
      < T1: NPVAR{NPVARrec1} >

      COMP
         <
          C1: VERBrec1.subc = beVerb AND
              NPVARrec1.definite = indef 
          A1: NPrec1 := AUX_NPONthereTHERE;
              NPrec1.number := NPVARrec1.number
         >
      DECOMP
         <
          C1: ( VERBrec1.subc = beVerb OR 
                VERBrec1.thetavp IN [vp010, vp012] ) AND
              NPVARrec1.definite = indef AND
              NPrec1 = AUX_NPONthereTHERE EXCEPT FOR [number] AND
              NPrec1.number = NPVARrec1.number
          A1: @
         >

   SUBRULE (* 2. NP *)
      < T1: NP{NPrec2}[mu6] >
      < T1: NP{NPrec2}[mu6] >

      COMP
         <
          C1: VERBrec1.subc = beVerb AND
              NPrec2.definite = indef 
          A1: NPrec1 := AUX_NPONthereTHERE;
              NPrec1.number := NPrec2.number
         >
      DECOMP
         <
          C1: ( VERBrec1.subc = beVerb OR 
                VERBrec1.thetavp IN [vp010, vp012] ) AND
              NPrec2.definite = indef AND
              NPrec1 = AUX_NPONthereTHERE EXCEPT FOR [number] AND
              NPrec1.number = NPrec2.number
          A1: @
         >
>
&

\end{verbatim}
\newpage
\begin{description}
\item[* name:] TThereSubjInsertion
\item[* relevant examples:]\mbox{}\\
(anal.) -- did come a man $\rightarrow$ There did come a man;\\
-- be some doubts about the plan $\rightarrow$ there be some doubts about the 
plan
\item[* language:] (Dutch, English, Spanish) English
\item[* kind of rule:] (M-rule, S-rule, Transferrule) M-rule
\item[* subgrammar:]  XPPROPtoClause
\item[* class:] (name of rule, transformation or filter class) 
TC\_ObjectOKrules
\item[* date of creation:] 22-MAR-1988
\item[* author:] margreet sanders
\item[* copied from:] 
\item[* modification:]\mbox{}
\begin{enumerate}
\item date: ../../..\ \ \ \ \ author:
\item date: ../../..\ \ \ \ \ author:
\end{enumerate}
\item[* task:] To insert the dummy subject `there' when there is no subject. 
Only allowed for the verb `be'; in analysis also allowed for a restricted set 
of verbs, probably best 
characterised by their being ergative (come, appear, etc). The direct object 
must be indefinite, 
and not universally quantifying ($^{*}$ There was everyone in the garden).
The NP over `there' gets the same number as the object NP.
\item[\ \ strategy:] (also: deviations from accepted proposals)
\item[\ \ rejected alternatives:]
\item[\ \ clarification of variables:]
\item[\ \ clarification of the splitting up into subrules:]dna
\item[\ \ clarification of the collapsing of rules:] (e.g. clarification of parameters)
\item[\ \ differences analytical/generative version:] none
\item[\  \ suggested semantics:]
\item[\ \ presuppositions:]\mbox{}
\begin{enumerate}
\item dictionary:\\

\item rules:\\

\end{enumerate}
